{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\WINDOWS\\\\system32\\\\cmd.exe\" /c \"type C:\\\\code\\\\claude\\\\skills\\\\code-review\\\\principles_cross_cutting.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\WINDOWS\\\\system32\\\\cmd.exe\" /c \"type C:\\\\code\\\\claude\\\\skills\\\\code-review\\\\principles_cross_cutting.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Code-review principles — cross-cutting\n\n**Apply first.** Every cluster lens (correctness, architecture, maintainability) is downstream of this — §24 verifies that the diff is grounded in reality before any other lens can be meaningfully applied, and §24-inward (the reviewer-grounding gate below) verifies that *your own findings* are grounded before any of them ship.\n\nThese principles were distilled from the scout 0.4.0 stability cycle, where code that *looked* correct silently did the wrong thing — DB written to a container WORKDIR instead of the bind mount, fastembed silently falling back to CPU while reporting \"GPU\", `scout status` traceback dismissed as non-fatal for hours. The principles are the gaps automated tests don't catch.\n\nNumbering is flat 1–27 across the four cluster files so cross-references resolve.\n\n---\n\n## 24. AI development shortcomings — verify, don't trust the prose\n\n**Rule:** AI-generated code (including this session's) ships with characteristic failure modes that don't surface as compile errors or test failures: hallucinated APIs, plausible-but-wrong reasoning, local-optimal-global-blind edits, defending output instead of re-verifying when challenged, confidence tone that doesn't track accuracy, and stopping at \"looks plausible\" instead of \"verified\". Scan for these explicitly when reviewing any AI-authored diff. Treat AI output as evidence, not truth.\n\n**Why:** The prose register is uniformly confident regardless of grounding. A reviewer reading *\"we use the canonical X pattern here\"* cannot tell whether X exists in the codebase, was hallucinated from a similar-looking project, or was misremembered from training data — without checking. The verification cost *is* the principle. Automated checks don't catch hallucinated APIs that happen to typecheck, plausible reasoning that misses the actual constraint, or local edits that diverge from established conventions (§20). Only a deliberate scan does.\n\n**Example (scout 0.4.0):** A session \"refactored\" the embedder to call `EmbedderRegistry.register_provider()` — a method that did not exist. The diff compiled (Python is duck-typed; the call sat inside an exception path the smoke test never exercised), and the prose justification was plausible (\"registries are the common pattern for this\"). Caught only at manual review by grepping the symbol. Now standard practice: every new method/attribute reference in an AI-authored diff is grepped before the diff is declared done.\n\n**How to scan for AI-specific shortcomings:**\n\n- **Hallucinated references:** every new import, method, attribute, flag, env var, config key — does it exist? Grep before trusting. **For third-party library APIs specifically**, use the Context7 MCP (`mcp__context7__resolve-library-id` → `mcp__context7__get-library-docs`) to fetch current, version-pinned docs. Training data goes stale; library APIs deprecate and rename; \"this method exists\" from memory is the same posture as \"trust me\". Context7's whole purpose is to close this gap.\n- **Plausible-but-ungrounded reasoning:** prose justifications that sound right but cite no file, test output, or observed behaviour. Demand the citation; absence of one is the concern.\n- **Local-optimal, global-blind:** one file improved beautifully while diverging from the surrounding convention (§20). Look across, not just within.\n- **Defend-vs-verify:** if a diff is the response to \"are you sure?\", check whether it *re-verified* (read the file, ran the test) or *re-asserted* (rewrote the prose more confidently). The latter is a tell.\n- **\"Almost done\" / \"should work\":** the gap between \"looks plausible\" and \"verified\" is where AI output most often fails. Anything left in the first state is a candidate concern, not a pride entry.\n- **Session-narrative in source comments:** agents fresh-context-narrate their work into comments — version refs (*\"0.8.9 closes the gap…\"*), branch walkthroughs, *\"I rewrote this because…\"* prose. This describes the agent's *process*, not the code's *behaviour*. Cross-listed under §16; flag wherever it appears.\n\n**Claude-specific failure mode:** Reviewing one's own diff is the failure mode this principle exists to defend against. The bias toward *\"of course it's right, I just wrote it\"* is exactly what `--fresh` mode is for. When reviewing AI output (yours or another session's) inline, this principle is the first lens applied, not the last — because every other lens depends on the diff being grounded in reality before it can be assessed against architecture, performance, or maintainability.\n\n---\n\n## Reviewer-grounding gate — §24 turned inward\n\n**Rule:** Your own draft findings are AI output with the same characteristic failure modes as the diff under review. Before emitting any Concern, name the evidence you checked. Acceptable evidence: `file:path:line` read, grep result (pattern + match count), Scout symbol lookup, Context7 doc fetch, or a pre-flight output row. *\"It reads as wrong\"* without a citation is not evidence; do not emit the Concern.\n\n**Why:** The whole point of §24 is that confident prose does not track accuracy. That applies to the reviewer just as much as to the diff. The most expensive class of false-positive code review is the BLOCKER that turns out, on second read, to be a misread of the surrounding code — wasted dev cycles, eroded trust in the reviewer, signal-to-noise loss across every future review. Forcing every Concern to cite its check is the cheapest defence: if you can't cite, you don't know.\n\n**The gate:**\n\n1. Draft your Concerns against the diff using the lens you're applying.\n2. For each drafted Concern, ask: *\"What did I read / grep / look up that established this?\"*\n3. If the answer names a specific channel (file + line, grep hit, doc fetch, pre-flight row) → keep, include the citation in the report.\n4. If the answer is *\"it reads as wrong\"* / *\"this pattern is usually a bug\"* / *\"the prose register suggests…\"* → either go check (read, grep, look up), or drop the finding.\n\n**Companion gate — warrant.** Grounding alone is not enough. Some findings are technically grounded (you can cite the line) but answer no version of *\"what would disappoint the CTO?\"* — *\"the docstring could mention X\"*, *\"consider adding a property-based test\"*, *\"`max_len` has no input validation\"* on a small additive parameter. These are quality-debt observations, not Concerns. On a clean diff, empty Concerns is the honest signal; padding the section with grounded-but-warrantless findings is rubber-stamping in reverse.\n\n**Reviewer-specific failure modes (from baseline observation):**\n\n| Failure mode | Counter |\n|---|---|\n| Over-emission once a real defect is found | Adjacent code is in scope iff the diff touches it. The framing question is *\"would this break in production?\"*, not *\"what else might be wrong?\"*. |\n| Severity inflation as emphasis | Severity is calibrated to *user impact*. Use the verdict body or Open questions to surface emphasis; do not inflate `[BLOCKER]`. |\n| False-positive padding on clean diffs | Empty Concerns is correct output on a clean diff. *\"To balance the report\"* / *\"standard quality debt\"* / *\"as a small nit…\"* are red flags. |\n| Returning before aggregation in `--deep` / `--fresh` | The deliverable is the aggregated report, not the dispatch. Every `<task-notification>` must be collected before turn-end. |\n\n**Where this gate lives in the workflow:** Phase 4 of the SKILL.md phased workflow. The lens scan (Phase 3) drafts; the grounding gate (Phase 4) prunes; the report (Phase 5) ships only what survives.\n\n---\n\n## Project `CLAUDE.md` and docs consistency\n\n**Rule:** The diff is checked against the nearest project `CLAUDE.md` — its invariants and conventions — and the documents that file routes to (architecture, methodology, decisions). Three questions, each answered with a citation or an explicit no: does the diff **break** a stated invariant, **contradict** a documented decision, or **leave one stale** (the code moved and the doc now describes a replaced gate, a removed command, an old layout)? A broken invariant is a `[BLOCKER]`; a doc left stale is an `[ADVISORY]` naming the section to update.\n\n**Why:** Implementation holes hide between the lines. A cluster lens reads the lines it is shown, but the contract those lines are supposed to honour lives in the project's own docs — a plan item never built, or a seam between two modules that each read correctly, leaves no line to flag. Observed: `docs/architecture.md` asserting wiring that does not exist, and a stale doc-comment describing a replaced gate, both shipped past lens reviews that read only the diff. This is the one lens that reads what the author was supposed to be consistent WITH, not just what they wrote.\n\n**How to apply:**\n\n- **Locate the nearest project `CLAUDE.md`** for the changed paths — walk up from each changed file to the first directory holding one. None in the reviewed tree → this lens has nothing to check; say so only if the diff itself adds a new project root.\n- **Read the documents it routes to** — architecture, methodology, decisions — the sections the changed paths fall under, not necessarily the whole file.\n- **Classify every finding**: break (the diff violates a stated invariant or convention), contradict (the diff implements the opposite of a documented decision), stale (the diff changes behaviour a doc still describes). Every finding cites the doc's `file:line` — *\"the doc probably says X\"* is the §24 failure mode pointed at the reviewer; read the section first.\n- **Severity**: a broken invariant is a `[BLOCKER]` (it violates a hard contract the project has declared); a stale doc is an `[ADVISORY]`. Findings land as Concerns with the doc line as the cited evidence, suggested fix included.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
